None of the built-in commands return the matched text — InJournal/WaitForJournal only return a bool (or the needle you passed, not the message). Let me check the Engine.Journal object for direct access.

None of the built-in journal commands hand you the message text:

Command	Returns
InJournal(text, ...)	bool — just whether it matched
WaitForJournal(text, ...)	bool — just whether it matched
WaitForJournal([array], ...)	(index, text) — but text is the needle you passed in, not the journal message
To get the actual string you read Engine.Journal directly. It's a CircularBuffer<JournalEntry> (capacity 1024), and each JournalEntry has:

Text, Name, Serial, SpeechHue, SpeechType, SpeechFont,
SpeechLanguage, Cliloc, Arguments, ID
GetEntireBuffer() returns the whole thing as a JournalEntry[].

Get the text of a message you're waiting for
from Assistant import Engine

def WaitForJournalEntry(needle, timeout = 3000, author = "", hue = -1):
    '''Waits for needle, returns the JournalEntry.Text of the newest match, or None.'''
    if not WaitForJournal(needle, timeout, author, hue):
        return None

    needle = needle.lower()
    author = author.lower() if author else ""

    entries = Engine.Journal.GetEntireBuffer()
    # newest first, so we grab the one that just arrived
    for i in xrange(len(entries) - 1, -1, -1):
        je = entries[i]
        if needle not in je.Text.lower():
            continue
        if author and (je.Name or "").lower() != author:
            continue
        if hue != -1 and je.SpeechHue != hue:
            continue
        return je.Text
    return None
Usage:

msg = WaitForJournalEntry("You play a tune", 5000)
if msg != None:
    SysMessage("Got: " + msg)
Parse without waiting — scan the buffer yourself
def FindJournal(needle, author = "", hue = -1, newest = True):
    needle = needle.lower()
    author = author.lower() if author else ""

    entries = Engine.Journal.GetEntireBuffer()
    order = xrange(len(entries) - 1, -1, -1) if newest else xrange(len(entries))

    matches = []
    for i in order:
        je = entries[i]
        if needle not in je.Text.lower():
            continue
        if author and (je.Name or "").lower() != author:
            continue
        if hue != -1 and je.SpeechHue != hue:
            continue
        matches.append(je)

    return matches          # list of JournalEntry, newest first
Then parse the string with plain Python:

hits = FindJournal("You create the item")
if hits:
    text = hits[0].Text
    # e.g. "You create the item and put it in your backpack."
    parts = text.split("and put it in your")
    SysMessage("text length = " + str(len(text)))

Things worth knowing
ClearJournal() does not clear the array. It only advances the named read offset ("main" by default), which is what InJournal/FindAny search from. GetEntireBuffer() ignores offsets entirely and returns everything still in the ring buffer. So after a ClearJournal(), InJournal sees only new messages, but GetEntireBuffer() still returns the old ones too — filter by what you just waited for, or snapshot the array before waiting.
Matching is substring + case-insensitive in the built-ins (je.Text.IndexOf(text, CurrentCultureIgnoreCase) >= 0). Mirror that with needle in je.Text.lower().
Multiple named buffers: InJournal(..., buffer="mybuf") / ClearJournal("mybuf") maintain independent read offsets, so you can track several conversation threads at once. GetEntireBuffer() is shared across all of them though.
Cliloc messages: for system messages, je.Cliloc and je.Arguments hold the cliloc ID and its args, and je.Text is the already-resolved text — so prefer je.Text for parsing.
Author can be a serial/alias — the built-ins call ResolveSerial(author) then look up the entity's Name. If you filter yourself, resolve it first:
from ClassicAssist.Data.Macros.Commands import AliasCommands
name = Engine.Mobiles.GetMobile(AliasCommands.ResolveSerial("somealias")).Name
